  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The paper is entirely conceptual and qualitative, offering a theoretical framework and metaphorical heuristics rather than empirical testing or quantitative modeling tools.",
      "what": "The paper presents a conceptual and epistemological analysis of transport decarbonisation, applying Daoist philosophy to critique dominant optimization and prediction paradigms. It uses three core concepts—wu-wei (non-coercive action), qi (vital circulation), and yin-yang (dynamic balance)—to reframe mobility as circulation and modeling as an interpretive practice. The theoretical framework is illustrated using the 15-minute city as a primary example, alongside cases from electric vehicles, road freight, aviation, and maritime governance.",
      "why": "It introduces a non-Western, process-oriented theoretical framework that challenges the assumption that transport systems are determinable objects yielding to top-down control. This offers planners a conceptual vocabulary to navigate persistent policy failures like rebound effects, implementation gaps, and social backlash by shifting from rigid prediction to adaptive, relational governance."
    }
